diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The Linux netfilter (iptables) FORWARD chain on router6, specifically three rules matching inbound interface `eth2`.

**Mechanism:** router6 is the sole gateway between the external routed network and the mswitch2 LAN segment (switch9/10/11, hosting pc15–pc18 and laptop4–5 on the 172.18.51.x subnet). Its interface `eth2` carries the `r6_r3` link — the only transit path from the rest of the topology (via router3) into this LAN. Three iptables rules have been applied to the FORWARD chain: one dropping TCP, one dropping UDP, and a catch-all dropping everything else (which includes ICMP). Any packet arriving from router3 on eth2 and destined for the mswitch2 subnet is silently dropped at router6 before it can be forwarded. Intra-subnet traffic (pc15 ↔ pc16 ↔ pc17 ↔ pc18) never transits router6, so it flows normally through mswitch2's Layer-2 switching — explaining why those hosts can reach each other while all external sources see complete silence.

**Localization:** router6, `eth2` (r6_r3 link to router3), iptables FORWARD chain.

**Root cause:** Three blanket DROP rules on router6's FORWARD chain for `-i eth2` block all forwarded traffic arriving from the external network, making the entire 172.18.51.x subnet unreachable from every external source.

**Fix:** Remove the three FORWARD DROP rules on router6: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, and `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`. Traffic from the external network will then be forwarded normally through router6 to the mswitch2 subnet.
=== END ===